window.DECK_NOTES={"cover": "Open by answering the thinking question from the end of Lecture 1: \"When your phone sends a photo over Wi-Fi, what physically travels through the air?\" Collect two or three answers. Most students will say \"bits\" or \"data\".\n\nReveal: bits cannot travel as bits. What travels is a signal: an electromagnetic wave whose properties (amplitude, frequency, phase) are changed in a pattern that represents the bits. Today we learn to describe signals precisely, and to see why a faster bit rate needs more bandwidth.\n\nThe orange curve on this slide starts as a pure sine wave and gains extra ripples (harmonics) towards the right: that is the story of today's lecture in one picture.", "objectives": "Read the five goals. Tell students the checkpoints today include short calculations, so they should keep a calculator ready (phone calculators are fine).\n\nBridge from Lecture 1: last time we saw that a message becomes bits and that the physical layer \"sends the bits\". Today we open that box: what does the physical layer actually put on the wire?\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Name one thing that travels along a fibre optic cable.\" Expected: light (pulses or waves of light).", "datasignal": "Key distinction: data is what we want to send; a signal is the physical form in which we send it.\n\nAnalog data: takes any value in a continuous range. Your voice is pressure that varies smoothly; a thermometer reading changes smoothly.\nDigital data: takes a limited set of values. A text file is a sequence of characters, each a pattern of bits.\n\nThe four boxes on the right are the roadmap for Lectures 5 to 8:\n- Analog data to digital signal (Lecture 5): your voice in a phone call is sampled and turned into bits.\n- Digital data to digital signal (Lecture 6): bits sent as voltage levels on an Ethernet cable.\n- Digital data to analog signal (Lecture 7): bits carried on a radio wave, as in Wi-Fi and 4G.\n- Analog data to analog signal (Lecture 8): voice carried directly on an FM radio wave.\n\nAnalogy: data is the letter you write; the signal is the vehicle (truck, boat, aeroplane) that carries it. The same letter can travel in different vehicles.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Watching YouTube on mobile data: which box?\" Expected: digital data to analog signal (bits on a radio wave).", "analogdigital": "Point at the two plots.\n\nAnalog: like a dimmer switch on a light. You can set any brightness between off and full; there are infinitely many positions.\nDigital: like an ordinary on/off switch. Only two positions, and the light jumps between them.\n\nCommon misconception: \"digital means computer, analog means old\". Not true. Wi-Fi carries digital data, but the radio wave itself is an analog signal. We will see that combination in Lecture 7.\n\nTeaching cue: ask students to look at the digital plot and read off the bits, taking high as 1 and low as 0. They should get 1 0 1 1 0 0 0 1.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A clock with hands versus a clock with digits: which is analog?\" Expected: the clock with hands (the hand moves through every position).", "periodic": "Periodic: like the seasons or a ceiling fan. The same pattern repeats at a regular interval. The dashed lines on the top plot mark one period each.\n\nAperiodic: like the traffic on a road or a conversation. It changes, but never in a fixed repeating pattern.\n\nWhy does data communication use periodic analog signals? Because a pure repeating wave (a carrier) is easy to generate, easy to filter and occupies a narrow band of frequencies. We then change its properties to carry data (Lecture 7).\n\nWhy are digital data signals aperiodic? Because real data does not repeat. A file is not 0101 0101 forever; if it were, it would carry almost no information.\n\nWording note: an aperiodic signal changes constantly, but never in a pattern that repeats.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Your heartbeat on an ECG monitor: periodic or aperiodic?\" Expected: approximately periodic. Good moment to note that real signals are rarely perfectly periodic.", "check1": "ANSWERS\nQ1: Analog data. Temperature can take any value in a range.\nQ2: Digital data carried on an analog signal (a radio wave). Covered in Lecture 7.\nQ3: The tuning fork's note is periodic (a near-perfect sine wave); speech is aperiodic.\n\nTeaching cue: if students answer \"digital signal\" for Q2, remind them that anything travelling through the air as a radio wave is an analog signal, even when it carries bits.", "sine": "Let the animation run for a few seconds before speaking.\n\nExplain: imagine a point moving around a circle at constant speed. If you look at its height only (the dashed line), the height goes up and down smoothly. Plot that height against time, and you get a sine wave.\n\nThis mapping gives students an intuition for all three parameters:\n- Peak amplitude A = the radius of the circle (a bigger circle gives a taller wave).\n- Frequency f = how many full turns per second (faster turning gives more cycles per second).\n- Phase φ = the angle at which the point starts at time zero.\n\nWhy sine waves matter: they are the building blocks. Later today we will build a square wave (digital signal) out of sine waves. Every signal can be described as a sum of sine waves.\n\nTeaching cue: write s(t) = A sin(2πft + φ) on the board and label each symbol. Keep it visible for the next four slides.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"If the point turns 5 times per second, what is f?\" Expected: 5 Hz.", "amplitude": "Analogy: amplitude is the volume knob. Turning it up makes the sound louder but does not change the note.\n\nPoint at the plot: both waves cross zero at the same moments and complete the same number of cycles. Only the height differs.\n\nLocal example: the electricity in this room is a sine wave. Its nominal value is 220 V, but that is the rms value (a kind of effective average). The actual peak is about 311 V. Students do not need rms in this course; it is mentioned only so the number does not confuse them.\n\nDC: a battery is the simplest case, a constant 1.5 V. Its frequency is zero because it never changes.\n\nLink forward: in Lecture 7, ASK (amplitude shift keying) represents bits by switching between two amplitudes.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A signal swings between +5 V and −5 V. What is its peak amplitude?\" Expected: 5 V (not 10 V; peak-to-peak would be 10 V).", "frequency": "Analogy: frequency is how many times a ceiling fan blade passes a point per second; period is the time between two passes.\n\nWalk down the table, connecting to things students know:\n- Mains: 50 cycles per second, so each cycle takes 1/50 = 0.02 s = 20 ms.\n- 1 kHz: an audible tone. Play a 1 kHz tone on a phone app if available.\n- FM radio around 100 MHz: one cycle every 10 nanoseconds.\n- Wi-Fi at 2.4 GHz: 2.4 billion cycles per second; one cycle lasts less than half a nanosecond.\n\nUnit trick: notice the matched pairs at the bottom. If the period is in milliseconds, the frequency comes out in kilohertz; microseconds go with megahertz; nanoseconds with gigahertz. This avoids most calculation mistakes.\n\nThe last bullet sets up later ideas: a sudden jump (like the edge of a digital signal) needs very high frequencies to draw. That is why digital signals need a lot of bandwidth.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A signal repeats every 4 ms. What is its frequency?\" Expected: 1/4 ms = 0.25 kHz = 250 Hz.", "workedfreq": "Solve both on the board before revealing the right side.\n\nProblem 1: convert first. 100 ms = 100 × 10^-3 s = 0.1 s. Then f = 1/0.1 = 10 Hz. Shortcut: 1/(100 ms) = 0.01 kHz = 10 Hz.\n\nProblem 2: 1/(2.4 × 10^9) = 0.4167 × 10^-9 s. Using the unit pairs: 1/GHz gives ns, and 1/2.4 = 0.417, so T = 0.417 ns.\n\nCommon mistakes to warn about:\n- Forgetting to convert milliseconds to seconds (giving 0.01 Hz instead of 10 Hz).\n- Writing the period as 2.4 ns (multiplying instead of dividing).\n\nSignal Lab: the Calculators tab converts period to frequency, so students can check their answers.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A 5 GHz signal: period?\" Expected: 0.2 ns.", "phase": "Analogy: runners on a circular track. They all run at the same speed (same frequency), but they started at different points on the track. The starting point is the phase.\n\nWalk the four panels:\n- 0°: the wave starts at zero and goes up.\n- 90°: a quarter cycle ahead, so it starts at the peak.\n- 180°: half a cycle ahead, so it starts at zero going down. This is the exact upside-down version of 0°.\n- 270°: three quarters ahead, so it starts at the bottom.\n\nConversion: degrees to radians, multiply by π/180. So 60° = π/3 rad.\n\nWhy phase matters: in Lecture 7, PSK (phase shift keying) sends bits by flipping the phase, for example 0° for bit 0 and 180° for bit 1. Wi-Fi and 4G use phase heavily.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A wave is shifted by 1/3 of a cycle. What is the phase in degrees?\" Expected: 120°.", "wavelength": "Wavelength connects time to distance. While the wave completes one cycle, it moves forward by one wavelength.\n\nAnalogy: footprints. Frequency is how many steps you take per second; wavelength is the length of one stride. At a fixed walking speed, more steps per second means shorter strides.\n\nWork one example: Wi-Fi at 2.4 GHz in air: λ = (3 × 10^8) / (2.4 × 10^9) = 0.125 m = 12.5 cm.\n\nWhy engineers care: antenna size depends on wavelength. FM radio (3 m wavelength) needs long antennas (a car's whip antenna is about 75 cm, a quarter of 3 m). Wi-Fi and 5G antennas are tiny because their wavelengths are a few centimetres.\n\nNote: the speed depends on the medium. Signals in copper and fibre travel at roughly two thirds of the speed of light in vacuum. This value returns when we calculate bit length later today and propagation delay in Lecture 3.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Does a higher-frequency signal have a longer or shorter wavelength?\" Expected: shorter.", "check2": "Give students three minutes with calculators.\n\nANSWERS\nQ1: f = 1 / 0.5 ms = 2 kHz (2000 Hz).\nQ2: 90 × π/180 = π/2 rad.\nQ3: λ = 3 × 10^8 / 5 × 10^9 = 0.06 m = 6 cm.\n\nTeaching cue: for Q1, students who answer 0.002 Hz have inverted the units. Show the unit-pair trick again: 1/ms = kHz.", "domains": "Two ways to look at the same signal.\n\nTime domain: we watch the signal change over one second. Good for seeing shape.\nFrequency domain: we list which frequencies are present and how strong each one is. For one sine wave, that list has one entry, so the plot is one spike.\n\nWalk the three rows:\n- DC 5 V: never changes, so frequency 0. The spike sits at 0 Hz with height 5.\n- 8 Hz sine with peak 5 V: one spike at 8 Hz, height 5.\n- 16 Hz sine: the spike moves to 16 Hz. Twice as many cycles in the time plot, spike twice as far right.\n\nNote that phase does not appear in this simple spectrum; the frequency-domain plot here shows only amplitude and frequency.\n\nAnalogy: a song versus its sheet music. Listening to the song is the time domain; the sheet music, which lists the notes, is the frequency domain.\n\nSignal Lab, tab 1: drag the frequency slider and watch the spike move.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A spike at 50 Hz with height 311. What signal is this?\" Expected: Bangladesh mains voltage (peak 311 V, 50 Hz).", "sinelive": "Live demo (website only). Left: time domain over a fixed 1-unit window; right: frequency domain, a spike at f with height A. Phase is in degrees, s(t) = A sin(2πft + φ).\n1. Click 8 Hz, then 16 Hz (the domains slide rows): twice as many cycles on the left, the spike moves twice as far right. Click DC 5 V: a flat line and a spike at 0 Hz of height 5.\n2. From 8 Hz, drag the phase slider to 90°: the wave now starts at its peak, but the spike does not move. Ask: \"Does phase show in this spectrum?\" Expected: no, only amplitude and frequency.\n3. Click 10 V DC + 6 Hz (the composite slide): the wave swings between 3 V and 17 V; spikes of 10 at 0 Hz and 7 at 6 Hz.\n4. Click T = 100 ms (workedfreq problem 1): T = 1/(10 Hz) = 0.1 s. Click 2.4 GHz: T = 0.417 ns and λ = 3 × 10⁸ / 2.4 × 10⁹ = 12.5 cm. Switch to cable 2×10⁸: λ drops to 8.33 cm.\n5. Click 1 + 5 kHz: two spikes, B = highest − lowest = 5 − 1 = 4 kHz (the bandwidth slide). Ask: \"Set f₂ = f, A₂ = A and phase 180°: what happens?\" Expected: the two sines cancel and the spike disappears.", "composite": "Single sine waves are useful but rare. Real signals (voice, music, data) are composite: mixtures of many sine waves.\n\nJean-Baptiste Joseph Fourier showed that any reasonably behaved periodic signal with period T can be written as a constant plus a sum of sines and cosines at the frequencies f, 2f, 3f, … where f = 1/T is the fundamental frequency.\n\nPrecision note: a periodic signal decomposes into discrete frequencies (a Fourier series). An aperiodic signal decomposes into a continuous range of frequencies (a Fourier transform), not into a list of periodic signals.\n\nWorked example on the right: 10 V DC plus a 7 V sine at 6 Hz.\n- Highest value: 10 + 7 = 17 V; lowest: 10 − 7 = 3 V.\n- Spectrum: a spike of height 10 at 0 Hz (the DC part) and a spike of height 7 at 6 Hz.\n\nPrism analogy: white light looks like one thing, but a prism shows it is many colours (frequencies) mixed together. A spectrum analyser does the same for signals.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A signal is 3 V DC plus a 2 V sine at 100 Hz. What is its maximum value, and where are the spikes?\" Expected: 5 V; spikes at 0 Hz (3) and 100 Hz (2).", "composelive": "Live demo (website only). s(t) = DC + sum of up to 5 sines A sin(2πft + φ). Top: time domain with max and min marked; bottom: amplitude spectrum (spike height = peak amplitude). Click a spike to see that one sine faintly in the time plot. Play turns the signal into sound (1 Hz on screen becomes 110 Hz; the beats preset uses 440 Hz + f instead so the 1 Hz beat stays audible).\n1. Click 10 V DC + 7 V, 6 Hz (the composite slide): the wave swings between min 3 V and max 17 V; spikes of 10 at 0 Hz and 7 at 6 Hz; B = 6 − 0 = 6 Hz. Ask: \"Set DC to 3 V and A to 2 V. Maximum?\" Expected: 5 V (the slide checkpoint).\n2. Click Beats 10 + 11 Hz and press Play: the loudness swells and fades once per second. The sum repeats every T = 1/gcd(10, 11) = 1 s although there is no 1 Hz spike. B = 11 − 10 = 1 Hz.\n3. Click Chord 4:5:6 and Play (440, 550, 660 Hz, a major chord). Then click Sawtooth: amplitudes 6, 3, 2, 1.5, 1.2 (proportional to 1/n) at 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 Hz. Click Triangle: only odd harmonics, amplitudes 8/n² with alternating sign (φ = 180°), already very close to a triangle with 3 terms.\n4. Click Same spectrum, new shape, then Swap phases a few times. Ask: \"The amplitude spectrum did not move. Is it the same signal?\" Expected: no; phases changed, so the shape changed (min −13.7 V instead of −9.5 V). The amplitude spectrum alone does not fix the shape; the phase spectrum (shown on the right) does.\n5. Set two components to the same f with phases 0° and 180° and equal A: the spike disappears. Ask: \"Why?\" Expected: the two sines cancel exactly.", "harmonics": "Let the animation cycle once (about 16 seconds) before explaining.\n\nWhat students see: with only the fundamental f, the signal is a plain sine wave. Adding 3f flattens the top a little. Adding 5f, 7f, 9f… makes the edges steeper and the top flatter. The dashed grey line is the ideal square wave the sum is approaching.\n\nKey ideas:\n- A digital signal (a square-like wave) is itself a composite signal. It is a sum of sine waves at f, 3f, 5f and so on.\n- Higher harmonics are weaker (height 1/n) but they are what make the sharp edges.\n- A perfect square wave, with vertical edges, would need infinitely many harmonics. Its exact bandwidth is infinite.\n\nWhy this matters: real channels only pass a limited range of frequencies. Whatever harmonics the channel removes, the receiver loses. This leads directly to the question at the end of the lecture: how much bandwidth does a bit rate need?\n\nSmall ripples near the edges never fully disappear with a finite number of harmonics (the Gibbs phenomenon). Mention only if students ask.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"If the fundamental is 1 kHz, what are the first three harmonics in a square wave?\" Expected: 1 kHz, 3 kHz, 5 kHz.", "harmonicslive": "Live demo (website only). Orange curve = sum of the first n odd harmonics; grey dashed = ideal ±1 square wave; bars = spectrum with heights 4/(πk).\n1. Click f only: a plain sine, peak 4/π = 1.273, 81.1% of the square wave's power.\n2. Click f, 3f, 5f (1 kHz), the slide's checkpoint: harmonics at 1, 3 and 5 kHz, amplitudes 1.273, 0.424, 0.255; 93.3% of the power. Signal bandwidth = highest − lowest = 5 − 1 = 4 kHz; a baseband channel must pass 0 to 5 kHz.\n3. Drag n from 3 up to 10: the edges get steeper. Ask: \"How many harmonics for a perfect square wave?\" Expected: infinitely many, so infinite bandwidth.\n4. Click 25: Gibbs. The peak stays at 1.179, about 9% of the 2 V jump, even though 99.2% of the power is kept. Ask: \"Do more harmonics remove the overshoot?\" Expected: no, the ripple gets narrower but not lower.\n5. Set f = 2 kHz and n = 3: highest = 10 kHz. Doubling f doubles every frequency and the bandwidth. Note: as bits 1010… this is N = 2f, which links to the bwrate slide (B ≈ N/2, 3N/2, 5N/2).", "lab": "Give students about 5 minutes with the lab on their phones or laptops, in pairs.\n\nSuggested tasks to write on the board:\n1. In tab 1, set f = 0. What happens to the wave and to the spike? (Flat line; spike moves to 0 Hz.)\n2. In tab 2, how many harmonics until the wave \"looks square\" to you? What bandwidth does that need with f = 1 Hz?\n3. In tab 3, keep N = 16 bps and lower B until bits break. Write down that B. Then double N to 32 bps and repeat. What happened to the B where it breaks?\n\nExpected discovery from task 3: when the bit rate doubles, the bandwidth at which bits start to fail roughly doubles too. This previews the rule B ≈ N/2 later in the lecture.\n\nNote for the instructor: the lab page is private until you share it. Before class, open it, choose Share, and make the link available to students (or display it from your own screen).\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): ask two pairs to report their answers to task 3.", "drawwavelive": "Live demo (website only). Draw one period in the white box with the mouse or a finger. The widget finds its Fourier series (DFT on 256 samples): bars = amplitude of DC, f, 2f … 20f with f = 1/T. The orange curve is DC + the first N harmonics; the panel shows the share of power kept and the RMS error.\n1. Click Square with N = 1: a plain sine of amplitude 1.273 = 4/π, 81.1 % of the power. Set N = 5: bars only at f, 3f, 5f (1.273, 0.424, 0.255, i.e. 4/(πn)), 93.3 %, the same numbers as the harmonics slide. Even harmonics are zero.\n2. Drag N to 20: still only 98 %, and the verdict says 99 % needs more than 20 harmonics. Ask: \"Why so many?\" Expected: the jumps (sharp corners) need high frequencies.\n3. Click Triangle: N = 1 already keeps 98.6 %, and 99 % needs only N = 3 (amplitudes fall as 1/n²). Smooth shape, few harmonics, narrow bandwidth.\n4. Click Sawtooth (amplitudes 0.637, 0.318, 0.212 … = 2/(πn), every harmonic present), then Narrow pulse: the bars hardly shrink at all (0.124, 0.122, 0.118 …) and 20 harmonics keep only 91 %. Ask: \"Which needs the widest bandwidth?\" Expected: the narrow pulse; the narrower the pulse, the flatter and wider its spectrum.\n5. Click Clear and let a student draw a smooth hill, then a shape with a sharp spike (or click Heartbeat: 90 % needs N = 16). Compare how many harmonics each needs.", "bandwidth": "Definition: bandwidth is how wide the spectrum is, highest frequency minus lowest frequency.\n\nAnalogy: the number of lanes on a road is not where the road goes but how wide it is. Bandwidth is the width of the frequency road.\n\nWork the example on the right: the signal contains frequencies from 1,000 Hz to 5,000 Hz, so its bandwidth is 4,000 Hz. Note that the bandwidth says nothing about which frequencies, only how wide the range is. A signal from 101,000 Hz to 105,000 Hz also has a bandwidth of 4,000 Hz.\n\nChannels have bandwidth too. A classic telephone line passes roughly 300 to 3,400 Hz. Anything outside that range is cut off. That is why voices sound thin on old phone calls: the high frequencies that give a voice its richness are removed.\n\nOrder matters: always highest minus lowest, so the answer is positive.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A signal occupies 88 MHz to 108 MHz. Bandwidth?\" Expected: 20 MHz (this is the whole FM broadcast band).", "filterlive": "Live demo (website only). Top: input (blue) and filter output (orange) in time. Bottom: the spectrum with an ideal filter (the channel) shaded orange; kept lines are orange, removed lines grey. Drag the orange edges on the spectrum or use the sliders. Power kept = power of the kept lines / total power (DC: a², sine: a²/2).\n1. Default: 0/1 V square wave at 1 kHz, low-pass f_H = 4 kHz. DC, f and 3f pass, the corners are rounded, power kept = 0.475 / 0.5 = 95%. Click Keep only f (2 kHz): just DC plus a 1 kHz sine, 90.5%. Click Keep f, 3f, 5f (6 kHz): 96.7%, the 'wide channel' of the digspectrum slide. Ask: \"Which harmonics survive a channel that passes up to 4 kHz (4f)?\" Expected: f and 3f.\n2. Click Remove DC (high-pass, f_L = 0.5 kHz): the same square shape, now centred on 0 (±0.5 V), and power kept drops to 50% because DC carried half the power. Drag f_L to 1.5 kHz: the fundamental (the slow trend) goes too and mostly the edges remain.\n3. Click Slide: 1–5 kHz and Band-pass: the bandwidth slide's spectrum, 1,000 to 5,000 Hz, B = 5,000 − 1,000 = 4,000 Hz. Drag the edges to 2,000 and 4,000 Hz: the output keeps 21 of 41 lines, B = 2,000 Hz.\n4. Click Telephone channel: a voice-like signal (harmonics of 200 Hz up to 6 kHz) through 300 to 3,400 Hz, channel B = 3,400 − 300 = 3,100 Hz. The 200 Hz line and everything above 3,400 Hz are removed; 89.9% of the power is kept. Ask: \"Why do voices sound thin on an old phone line?\" Expected: the high frequencies above 3,400 Hz that give a voice its richness are cut off.\n5. Ask: \"Is the output's bandwidth the same as the channel's?\" Expected: not necessarily; here the output runs 400 to 3,400 Hz, B = 3,000 Hz, because only the lines inside the band count.", "separatelive": "Live demo (website only). Top: the mixture in time (what one microphone records). Middle: the output of our cut (orange) against the true signal we want (dashed). Bottom: the mixture's amplitude spectrum, coloured by source. Error E = √(Σ(out − true)² / Σ true²): 0 % means perfectly recovered. The readout also says how much of each source got through. Click or drag on the top plot to cut in time, on the bottom plot to cut in frequency.\n1. Default: voice + 50 Hz mains hum (with its 150 Hz harmonic) + a 3 kHz whistle, and a time cut from 10 to 30 ms. Ask: \"Can we keep the voice by choosing a time window?\" Drag the window anywhere: E never gets below 62.5 %. Every cut keeps the same share of all three sources (10–30 ms keeps 50 % of each), because all three are present at every instant. Press Play Mixture and Play Output: the output is just shorter, not cleaner.\n2. Click Best frequency cut (175 to 1,100 Hz): E = 0.0 %, voice kept 100 %, hum 0 %, whistle 0 %. In frequency the three sources occupy separate places. Switch the goal to Mains hum (0 to 175 Hz) and Whistle (2,000 to 4,000 Hz): each comes out exactly. Play Output each time. Ask: \"Which earlier topic does this remind you of?\" Expected: a filter / tuning a radio / FDM (Lecture 9).\n3. Click Different times: two speakers who take turns (A in 0–20 ms, B in 20–40 ms) using the same frequencies, 1 and 2 kHz. The dashed outlines in the spectrum lie on top of each other. The default frequency cut 800 to 1,200 Hz keeps 80 % of A but also lets 20 % of B through, E = 63 %. Ask: \"Which domain should we use now?\" Click Best time cut (0 to 20 ms): E = 0.0 %. This is TDM (Lecture 9).\n4. Click Overlap: voice + noise. The best band 175 to 1,025 Hz lowers E from 34 % (no cut) to about 10 %, but not to 0: the noise inside the voice's band (about 9 % of it) stays. Neither domain separates signals that overlap in both time and frequency; that needs smarter methods such as speech enhancement.\n5. Conclusion to write on the board: separate in the domain where the signals do not overlap. Different frequencies → frequency domain (filters, FDM). Different times → time domain (gating, TDM).", "workedbw": "Solve each on the board.\n\n(a) Highest minus lowest: 900 − 100 = 800 Hz. Note we do not add up the components or count them; only the extremes matter.\n\n(b) Rearrange: f_low = f_high − B = 60 − 20 = 40 Hz. If all components have equal amplitude, the spectrum is a flat block from 40 to 60 Hz.\n\n(c) Centred means half the bandwidth on each side: 10 kHz / 2 = 5 kHz. So 1,000 − 5 = 995 kHz to 1,000 + 5 = 1,005 kHz. Neighbouring AM stations are spaced 10 kHz apart so their bands do not overlap. This returns in Lecture 8 (AM) and Lecture 9 (FDM).\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"A signal's lowest frequency is 2 kHz and its bandwidth is 6 kHz. Highest frequency?\" Expected: 8 kHz.", "twobw": "This is a frequent source of confusion, including among professionals.\n\nWhen an electrical engineer says \"this cable has 100 MHz of bandwidth\", they mean hertz: the cable passes frequencies up to about 100 MHz.\nWhen an ISP advertises \"100 Mbps bandwidth\", they mean bits per second: capacity.\n\nThe two are related: a wider frequency range lets you send more bits per second. But the relationship is not fixed. How many bits per second fit into a given number of hertz depends on how many signal levels you use and how much noise there is. Lecture 3 gives the exact limits: the Nyquist formula for noiseless channels and the Shannon capacity for noisy ones.\n\nCommon misconception: \"more hertz always means more bps\". It is true only if everything else (signal levels, noise) stays the same.\n\nAnalogy: the width of a road (hertz) limits how many cars per minute can pass (bits per second), but so do the speed limit and the weather (noise).\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Your router box says 'AC1200'. Is that hertz or bits per second?\" Expected: a rough total data rate (bits per second, about 1200 Mbps), not hertz.", "listenlive": "Live demo (website only). Nothing plays until you click; leaving the slide stops the sound and releases the microphone. Set the volume first. Top: waveform (25 ms); middle: spectrum 0 to 5 kHz in dB with the peak and the span within 30 dB of it; bottom: spectrogram (time to the right, frequency up, brighter = louder). Before Play, the displays show a preview computed in JS.\n1. Vowel \"aa\" (the default), press Play. The waveform repeats every T = 1/120 = 8.33 ms; the spectrum shows harmonics at 120, 240, 360 ... Hz; the tallest sit near F1 = 730 and F2 = 1,090 Hz. Click \"ee\". Ask: \"What moved and what stayed?\" Expected: the harmonic spacing (pitch, 120 Hz) stays; the formant peaks move to 270 and 2,290 Hz. That is how we tell vowels apart.\n2. Tone, click 1,000 Hz: one spike and one horizontal line, T = 1/1,000 = 1 ms. Drag to 4,000 Hz: the spike moves right, T = 0.25 ms. Click 300 Hz and 3,400 Hz: the two edges of the phone channel (bandwidth slide), B = 3,400 − 300 = 3,100 Hz.\n3. Chord: three sines, three spikes at 523, 659 and 784 Hz; B = 784 − 523 = 261 Hz (the readout shows a little more because each spike has a width).\n4. Chirp: a straight line rising from 200 to 4,000 Hz in 2 s (1,900 Hz per second). Press Freeze and point at it, then Resume. Whistle: the line curves up and down between 1,200 and 2,400 Hz.\n5. Noise, then Clap: power at every frequency; the readout B reaches the edge of the chart and says the phone cuts below 300 and above 3,400 Hz. A clap is a vertical stripe. Ask: \"Why is 'sss' hard to hear on a phone call?\" Expected: most of its energy is above 3,400 Hz, outside the 300 to 3,400 Hz telephone channel.\n6. Microphone, Start mic (needs a secure page such as https or localhost; if it is refused, the status line says why and the built-in sounds still work). Say a long \"aaa\" then \"eee\", whistle, and hiss \"sss\" with Phone band on.", "sourceslive": "Live demo (website only). Six sources that are not sound. Each shows the sensor, the samples in time (top), the amplitude spectrum (bottom), and what each domain tells you (cards below). The signals are realistic models computed in the browser, not recordings: say so if asked. Spectrum: Blackman window, average removed, so line heights are peak amplitudes in the signal's own unit; dB is relative to the tallest peak.\n1. Mains power (opens on the dB scale): 220 V rms → 311 V peak, 50 Hz, T = 20 ms (the checkpoint on the domains slide). Drag Non-linear loads from 0 to 100 %. In time the tops flatten only slightly; in frequency, lines appear at 150, 250, 350, 450 Hz. THD = √(V₃² + V₅² + V₇² + V₉²) / V₁ rises to 7.8 % at 100 % (IEEE 519 limit for low-voltage supply: 8 %). Ask: \"In which plot did you notice the distortion first?\" Expected: the spectrum.\n2. Heartbeat (ECG): 72 beats/min → f₀ = 72 / 60 = 1.2 Hz, T = 0.833 s. The spectrum is lines every 1.2 Hz (a periodic signal has discrete lines). Drag to 150 beats/min: the lines spread to every 2.5 Hz. Raise the 50 Hz interference to 0.2 mV: the trace becomes fuzzy, but in frequency it is one separate spike that a notch filter can remove. The time domain is still what a doctor reads (shape of P, QRS, T).\n3. Temperature: one reading per hour for 14 days. Frequency in cycles per day: 1 cycle/day = 1/86,400 s = 11.6 µHz. Peaks at 1 (day and night), 2 (the daily shape is not a pure sine) and about 0.2 (weather changing every 5 days). Ask: \"What would a peak at 1/365 cycles per day mean?\" Expected: the seasons, visible only with a year of data.\n4. Machine vibration: shaft at 1,470 rpm = 24.5 Hz, blade pass 5 × 24.5 = 122.5 Hz. The waveform looks like noise. Raise Bearing damage to 50 %: the RMS (a time-domain number) barely changes, but a new line appears at 3.58 × 24.5 = 87.7 Hz. This is how factories detect failing bearings weeks early.\n5. AM radio: the antenna voltage with 1 to 5 stations at 630, 750, 870, 990 and 1,110 kHz. With 5 stations the time plot is a jumble; in frequency each station is a separate 10 kHz slot. Tune to 750 kHz: message tones up to 4 kHz → B = 2 × 4 = 8 kHz (AM rule, Lecture 8). Separating them is FDM (Lecture 9).\n6. Earthquake: P waves at 6 km/s, S waves at 3.5 km/s. At 200 km, t_S − t_P = 23.8 s and d = 23.8 × 8.4 = 200 km. Drag the distance: the gap grows. The spectrum is continuous (a one-off event) and cannot tell when the waves arrived. This is the clearest example where the time domain wins.", "spatiallive": "Live demo (website only). An image is a signal over space instead of time: brightness against position. One row of pixels (bottom left) is an ordinary 1-D signal; its frequency is counted in cycles per image width. The whole image has a 2-D spectrum (top middle, log scale, DC in the centre): every point (u, v) is one stripe pattern with u cycles across and v cycles down. Top right is the image rebuilt from the kept coefficients (inverse 2-D FFT). Click either image to choose the row.\n1. Click Stripes, No filter: one stripe pattern = two dots at ±(16, 0), just as one sine = one spike. Drag u to 4 and to 32: the dots move out as the stripes get finer. Set v = 16: diagonal stripes, dots on the diagonal. Ask: \"Why two dots?\" Expected: a real cosine has a positive and a negative frequency (mirror twins).\n2. Click Cameraman, Low-pass, r = 24: kept 1,793 of 65,536 coefficients (2.7 %), the picture is blurred but recognisable, PSNR 23.6 dB. Drag r to 8 (very blurred, 19.8 dB) and 48 (27.3 dB). Ask: \"Which part of the image needs the high frequencies?\" Expected: the edges (coat, camera, tripod legs).\n3. High-pass, r = 24: only edges and texture remain. This is edge detection, the same idea as a high-pass filter on sound.\n4. Click Stripe noise: the cameraman with a stripe pattern added on every pixel (amplitude 45 grey levels). Choose Remove spikes (it finds the two spikes at ±(20, 12)): the stripes vanish and the error from the clean photo falls from 31.8 to 2.3 grey levels. In the space domain this noise covers every pixel and cannot be cut out; in frequency it is two points. Ask: \"Why does this work so well?\" Expected: the noise and the picture occupy different frequencies (as with the hum on the unmix slide).\n5. Keep strongest: 1 % of the coefficients gives 22.4 dB, 5 % gives 26.8 dB, 20 % gives 33.0 dB. The picture survives with far fewer numbers: the idea behind JPEG (which uses the DCT on 8 × 8 blocks).\n6. Your photo…: students can load any image from the PC (it stays in the browser; nothing is uploaded). Photos: scikit-image sample images \"camera\" and \"coffee\", both CC0.", "whichdomainlive": "Live demo (website only). Thirteen short questions, each with its signal in both domains. Read the question, let the class vote (hands up: time, frequency or both), then click. The plot that answers it gets an orange frame, and the lesson is added to the list on the right. \"Show all answers\" fills the whole list for revision.\nAnswers: 1 clap: time (spike at 1.3 s). 2 chord notes: frequency (262, 330, 392 Hz = C, E, G). 3 clipping: time (flat tops at ±1 V; the spectrum shows new harmonics but not the limit). 4 bandwidth: frequency (4,000 − 1,000 = 3,000 Hz). 5 remove 50 Hz hum from an ECG: frequency (notch). 6 read NRZ bits: time (10110010). 7 three stations on one cable: frequency (FDM at 100, 200, 300 kHz). 8 four users taking turns: time (TDM slots A B C D). 9 compress music: frequency (MP3/JPEG idea). 10 earthquake distance: time (t_S − t_P = 23.8 s → 200 km). 11 hidden bearing fault: frequency (line at 87.7 Hz). 12 rising whistle: both (needs a spectrogram). 13 heart rate: both (T = 0.833 s or f₀ = 1.2 Hz; time or frequency alone is also accepted).\nDiscussion points:\n1. The time domain is best for WHEN and HOW BIG: events, delays, peaks and limits, bits and their timing, TDM.\n2. The frequency domain is best for WHICH FREQUENCIES and HOW MUCH OF EACH: tones, bandwidth, filtering, FDM, compression, weak periodic parts in noise.\n3. Both views hold the same information (the Fourier transform can be undone, as the image slide showed); what changes is which question is easy to answer.\n4. For signals whose frequency changes over time (speech, music, chirps), use a time–frequency view: the spectrogram on the \"real sounds\" slide.", "fourierfamilylive": "Live demo (website only). Click any cell to see its formula, what it is used for and whether a computer can calculate it. The same example is used in every cell: a pulse 4 samples wide; the periodic versions repeat every 16. Press Next step on the right to follow a real signal from the analog world into a computer.\n\nTHEORY FOR THE LECTURER\n1. Four transforms, one idea. Every Fourier transform writes a signal as a sum of complex sinusoids e^(j2πft). Which version you use depends on two questions about the time signal: is time continuous or sampled, and is the signal periodic (or treated as periodic) or not?\n- Fourier series (FS): continuous, periodic with period T. Only frequencies n/T fit a whole number of times into T, so the spectrum is a set of lines cₙ at n/T. cₙ = (1/T)∫₀ᵀ x(t)e^(−j2πnt/T)dt. The harmonics slides of this lecture are Fourier series.\n- Fourier transform (FT): continuous, not periodic. Let T → ∞: the lines 1/T apart merge into a continuous spectrum X(f) = ∫x(t)e^(−j2πft)dt. The pulse slide is this: a pulse of width τ has |X(f)| = τ|sinc(fτ)|, first zero at 1/τ.\n- DTFT: samples x[n] = x(nTs), not periodic. X(f) = Σ x[n]e^(−j2πfn/fs). Because e^(−j2π(f+fs)n/fs) = e^(−j2πfn/fs), the spectrum repeats every fs. These copies are the images of Lecture 5: if the signal's band B exceeds fs/2, the copies overlap (aliasing). Hence fs > 2B.\n- DFT: N samples, treated as one period of a periodic signal. X[k] = Σₙ₌₀ᴺ⁻¹ x[n]e^(−j2πkn/N), k = 0 … N−1; inverse x[n] = (1/N)Σₖ X[k]e^(j2πkn/N). Bin k is at f = k·fs/N, so the resolution is Δf = fs/N = 1/(N·Ts) = 1/(observation time). The DFT values are samples of the DTFT of the N kept samples at f = k·fs/N.\n2. The duality rule: discrete (sampled) in one domain ⇔ periodic in the other. Periodic in time → line spectrum (FS). Sampled in time → periodic spectrum (DTFT). The DFT is sampled in both domains, so it is periodic in both: N numbers in, N numbers out. That is exactly why it is the one a computer can compute.\n3. The FFT is an algorithm, not a new transform. It computes exactly the DFT (same numbers, up to rounding) by splitting an N-point DFT into two N/2-point DFTs (even and odd samples) again and again (Cooley and Tukey, 1965). Cost: about (N/2)·log₂N complex multiplications instead of N². N = 1,024: 1,048,576 vs 5,120, about 205 times faster; N = 65,536: about 8,192 times faster. MATLAB fft, NumPy np.fft.fft and every audio visualiser call an FFT.\n4. Practical consequences of using the DFT on real signals: (a) leakage: a tone that does not fit a whole number of cycles in N samples spreads into neighbouring bins, because the DFT assumes the N samples repeat; a window (Hann, Blackman) reduces this, as on the real-world signals slide. (b) Resolution Δf = fs/N: two tones closer than about fs/N cannot be separated; more samples (a longer recording) are needed, not a faster fs. (c) Zero-padding gives a smoother-looking plot but no extra resolution.\n5. Related transforms students will meet: the DCT (real-valued cousin of the DFT; JPEG, and the MDCT in MP3 and AAC) and wavelets (JPEG 2000).\n\nDemo steps\n1. Click each cell in turn: Fourier series (lines, not periodic), Fourier transform (continuous), DTFT (continuous, copies every fs), DFT (lines and copies). Ask: \"Which two cells have a periodic spectrum, and what do they have in common in time?\" Expected: DTFT and DFT; both are sampled in time.\n2. Press Next step four times: FT (real sound) → sample → DTFT → keep N samples → DFT → FFT. At step 2 link to Nyquist (Lecture 5): the copies must not overlap.\n3. Drag N on the right from 8 to 65,536. At 1,024: 1,048,576 vs 5,120 multiplications. Ask: \"If one multiplication takes 1 ns, how long does a 65,536-point DFT take by the formula?\" Expected: 65,536² ns ≈ 4.3 s; the FFT needs (32,768 × 16) ns ≈ 0.52 ms.\n4. Ask: \"Does the FFT give a more accurate answer than the DFT?\" Expected: no, the same answer, only faster.", "stftlive": "Live demo (website only). Top: a 2 s signal with an orange window. Middle left: the FFT of that window only; middle right: one FFT of the whole 2 s. Bottom: the spectrogram, one FFT per window position placed side by side (colour = level in dB). Press Play and scan to hear the sound while the window moves (tap the slide background first if the arrow keys move a slider).\n\nTHEORY FOR THE LECTURER\n1. Why one FFT is not enough. The DFT of the whole recording tells which frequencies occur and how strongly, but its magnitude says nothing about when. The timing is not destroyed (the DFT can be inverted exactly); it is hidden in the phase, where it cannot be read. For signals whose content changes (speech, music, a cough) we want frequency against time.\n2. The short-time Fourier transform: X[m, k] = Σₙ x[n + mH] w[n] e^(−j2πkn/N). Cut the signal into frames of N samples, hop H apart (here H = N/4), multiply each frame by a window w[n] (here Hann) to reduce leakage, and take an FFT of each. |X[m, k]|² plotted with time m across and frequency k up is the spectrogram.\n3. The time-frequency trade-off (uncertainty principle). One frame lasts Δt = N/fs and its bins are Δf = fs/N apart, so Δt·Δf = 1: you cannot make both small. Short windows give sharp timing and blurry frequency; long windows give sharp frequency and blurry timing. It is the same law as the pulse slide (a pulse of width τ needs a bandwidth of about 1/τ). Speech recognisers use windows of 20 to 30 ms with a 10 ms hop, then map the bins onto the mel scale (mel spectrogram, MFCCs), which is the input to most speech and audio deep-learning models.\n4. Uses of the STFT in audio: speech recognition (phonemes change every few tens of ms), speech enhancement and noise removal (the cough occupies a time slice, the hum a frequency band; a mask on the spectrogram removes each, followed by the inverse STFT with overlap-add), music analysis (notes, chords, beats), birdsong and machine sounds, and the Gabor transform (an STFT with a Gaussian window).\n5. Images. An image has no time axis, so the plain 2-D DFT of the whole image is used for spatial frequency (as on the image slide). When position matters, images use local transforms as well, just not under the name STFT: JPEG applies a DCT to every 8 × 8 block (a short-space transform), texture analysis uses Gabor filters (Gaussian-windowed sinusoids, i.e. a 2-D STFT), JPEG 2000 uses wavelets, and CNNs learn their own local filters.\n6. Wavelets in one sentence: instead of one window length for all frequencies, they use short windows for high frequencies and long windows for low frequencies, which suits signals with fast transients and slow trends.\n\nDemo steps\n1. Speech-like, N = 256 (32 ms): the spectrogram shows the vowel \"aa\" (energy near 730 and 1,090 Hz), a gap, the vowel \"ee\" (270 Hz and 2,290 to 3,010 Hz), then the hiss of \"s\" (2.5 to 3.9 kHz). Drag the window position: the middle-left spectrum changes; the middle-right one (whole signal) never does. Ask: \"From the right-hand spectrum alone, can you tell which vowel came first?\" Expected: no.\n2. Press Play and scan: students hear each part while the window passes it.\n3. Melody, then drag N from 64 to 2,048. N = 64 (8 ms, Δf = 125 Hz): note starts are sharp but the harmonics are thick bands. N = 2,048 (256 ms, Δf = 3.91 Hz): very thin lines, but each note's start is smeared over a quarter of a second. Read the readout: Δt × Δf = 1 at every setting.\n4. Chirp (f = 300 + 1,600t Hz): one FFT of the whole signal is flat from 300 to 3,500 Hz; the spectrogram shows a rising line. Ask: \"What pitch is heard at 1 s?\" Expected: 1,900 Hz, readable only from the spectrogram.\n5. Hum + cough: the hum is horizontal lines (fixed frequency, all the time) and the cough is a vertical band (short time, all frequencies). Ask: \"How would you remove each one?\" Expected: the hum with a notch filter in frequency; the cough by muting that time slice; on a spectrogram both are just regions to mask.\n6. Finish with the table on the right: one FFT for steady signals, STFT for changing sound, 2-D FFT for whole images, local transforms (block DCT, Gabor, wavelets) for where-in-an-image, DCT/MDCT for compression.", "digital": "Two levels: high = 1, low = 0. Each signal element carries one bit.\n\nFour levels: each level represents a pair of bits (here 11, 10, 01, 00 from top to bottom). The second plot sends 16 bits in the same time the first plot sends 8.\n\nFormula: bits per level = log2(L). For L = 8, log2 8 = 3 bits. Students can check: 2^3 = 8.\n\nTrade-off: if the maximum voltage is fixed, more levels means smaller gaps between them. A small amount of noise can push the signal into the wrong level. That is why Shannon's limit (Lecture 3) ties the number of usable levels to the signal-to-noise ratio.\n\nReal-world: Gigabit Ethernet over copper uses five-level signalling (PAM-5); modern Wi-Fi uses very many \"levels\" in a two-dimensional form (QAM, Lecture 7).\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"How many levels are needed to send 4 bits per level?\" Expected: 2^4 = 16 levels.", "bitrate": "Digital signals are aperiodic, so period and frequency do not apply directly. We use parallel ideas:\n- Frequency ↔ bit rate (how many per second)\n- Period ↔ bit interval (how long each lasts)\n- Wavelength ↔ bit length (how much distance each occupies)\n\nPlot: 8 bits in one second gives 8 bps, and each bit lasts 1/8 = 0.125 s.\n\nFast Ethernet example: 100 Mbps means 100 × 10^6 bits per second. Bit interval = 1 / 10^8 = 10 ns. Bit length = 2 × 10^8 m/s × 10 × 10^-9 s = 2 m. So at any instant, a 100 m Ethernet cable holds about 50 bits \"in flight\". This idea becomes the bandwidth-delay product in Lecture 3.\n\nWarning on units: in networking, k, M and G are powers of 10 (1 Mbps = 1,000,000 bps), not powers of 2 as in file sizes.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Bit interval at 1 Gbps?\" Expected: 1 ns.", "workedrate": "Each example uses the same method: count what is sent per second, then multiply down to bits.\n\nText: 100 pages × 24 lines × 80 characters × 8 bits = 1,536,000 bps = 1.536 Mbps. (Downloading a 100-page text document in one second.)\n\nHD video: 1920 × 1080 = 2,073,600 pixels per frame; × 30 frames per second; × 24 bits per pixel = 1,492,992,000 bps ≈ 1.5 Gbps. That is far more than a typical home connection, which is why video is always compressed (often by a factor of 100 or more).\n\nVoice: 8,000 samples per second × 8 bits = 64,000 bps = 64 kbps. Same number as Lecture 1.\n\nTeaching cue: let students estimate first (\"more or less than 1 Mbps?\") before computing. Estimation builds intuition.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Uncompressed 4K video (3840 × 2160) at 30 frames/s and 24 bits per pixel?\" Expected: four times the HD figure, about 6 Gbps.", "digspectrum": "Connect back to the harmonics animation: a digital signal is a square-like wave, which is a sum of harmonics.\n\nExact spectrum versus significant spectrum:\n- To reproduce the signal perfectly, we would need infinitely many harmonics: infinite bandwidth. No channel has that.\n- Fortunately, the receiver does not need a perfect copy. It only needs to decide, for each bit interval, whether the signal is high or low. A few harmonics are usually enough. The range of frequencies that carry most of the shape is the significant spectrum.\n\nThe three plots show what happens to the same signal through channels of different width:\n- Sent: sharp.\n- Wide channel: rounded corners, but every bit is still clear.\n- Narrow channel: only the fundamental survives. The pattern is still visible here because the bits alternate, but with real, irregular data, bits would be lost.\n\nSignal Lab, tab 3 shows exactly this, with any bit pattern students choose.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Which harmonics survive a channel that passes up to 4f?\" Expected: f and 3f (5f is above 4f).", "pulselive": "Live demo (website only). Top: 0/1 V rectangular pulses of width τ, repeating every T. Bottom: the spectrum on a fixed 0 to 5 MHz axis. For a pulse train the lines sit at n/T with heights |cₙ| = (τ/T)·|sinc(nτ/T)| under the dashed sinc envelope; the red dashed line marks the first zero at 1/τ, and the shaded band is B ≈ 1/τ.\n1. Default τ = 1 µs, T = 4 µs: lines every f₀ = 1/T = 0.25 MHz, DC term c₀ = τ/T = 0.25, first zero at 1/τ = 1 MHz, and the line at 1 MHz is exactly zero.\n2. Click 0101… at 1 Mbps (τ = 1 µs, T = 2 µs): lines at 0.5, 1.5, 2.5 MHz; the even ones fall on the zeros. These are N/2, 3N/2, 5N/2 from the bwrate slide.\n3. Click Periodic vs aperiodic: T grows from 2 µs to ∞. The lines crowd together under the same envelope and become a continuous spectrum. Ask: \"What changed, the shape or the spacing?\" Expected: only the spacing 1/T; the envelope depends on τ alone. Periodic → discrete spikes, aperiodic → continuous spectrum, as on the composite slide.\n4. Click One bit at 1 Mbps (single pulse, τ = 1 µs): first zero 1 MHz. Click Narrower pulse (τ = 0.25 µs): first zero 4 MHz. Ask: \"Halve the pulse width: what happens to the bandwidth?\" Expected: it doubles; shorter pulses (faster bits) need more bandwidth.\n5. Click Digital data: 1,536 random bits of width τ. There is no period, so the measured spectrum is continuous but follows the same |sinc(fτ)| envelope with its first zero at 1/τ = N. Click it again for new bits; drag τ to see the spectrum stretch.", "bwrate": "Derivation, step by step on the board:\n1. The bit pattern that changes fastest is 0101 0101…\n2. One full cycle (one 0 and one 1) takes two bit intervals. So the square wave's fundamental frequency is f = N/2, where N is the bit rate.\n3. A channel must pass at least this fundamental for the receiver to see the alternation, so B_min ≈ N/2.\n4. If we also want the third harmonic (cleaner edges), the channel must pass up to 3f = 3N/2. With the fifth, 5N/2.\n\nWorked example: to send 1 Mbps, we need at least 500 kHz; 1.5 MHz for better shape; 2.5 MHz for better still.\n\nBig idea: required bandwidth grows in proportion to bit rate. Faster bits need wider channels. This is why 5G and Wi-Fi 6 use wide frequency channels (tens to hundreds of MHz).\n\nNote: N/2 here is a rough rule for this simple two-level signal. The exact limits come in Lecture 3 (Nyquist: N = 2B log2 L for a noiseless channel). Students who notice that N/2 and 2B are the same relationship deserve praise.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Minimum bandwidth for 10 Mbps using only the first harmonic?\" Expected: 5 MHz.", "bitsbwlive": "Live demo (website only). The bit pattern repeats forever, so its spectrum has harmonics every f₀ = N/L. The channel keeps only the harmonics at or below B (an ideal low-pass filter). The receiver samples at each bit centre and compares with ½ (green dot = right, red = wrong).\n1. Click 0101…, B = N/2 (N = 1 Mbps, B = 500 kHz): only the first harmonic of the square wave passes, a sine, yet all 8 bits are read.\n2. Click 0101…, B < N/2 (450 kHz): the harmonic at N/2 is cut, only the flat average remains, and 4 of 8 bits are wrong. Ask: \"What is the minimum bandwidth for 10 Mbps?\" Expected: 5 MHz; click 10 Mbps, 5 MHz to check.\n3. Click B = 3N/2 (1.5 MHz) and B = 5N/2 (2.5 MHz): the received shape gets closer to the square wave, as in the bwrate table.\n4. Click Lab: 16 / 12 (01001000, N = 16 Mbps, B = 12 MHz, as in the Signal Lab): 0 errors. Drag B down slowly: it still works below N/2 = 8 MHz (the verdict warns this pattern is lucky), and below 4 MHz the two 1s are lost. Then raise N with B fixed and watch errors appear.\n5. Click bits to make your own pattern. Ask: \"Which pattern needs the most bandwidth?\" Expected: alternating 0101…, the worst case on the slide. With this ideal channel any pattern is read once B ≥ N/2.", "baseband": "Two strategies for getting bits onto a medium.\n\nBaseband: put the digital signal on the wire as it is. Its spectrum starts near 0 Hz, so the channel must be low-pass (pass everything from 0 Hz up to some limit). A dedicated cable, like the Ethernet cable from your PC to a switch, works well.\n\nBroadband: the channel only passes a band of frequencies away from zero, for example 2.400 to 2.483 GHz for 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi. A digital signal cannot be put there directly; it must be modulated onto a carrier wave at that frequency. Different users or services can use different bands of the same medium (radio, a cable TV coaxial cable), which is how many networks share the air.\n\nAnalogy: baseband is a private driveway that starts at your door; broadband is a lane on a shared highway assigned to you.\n\nNote on terms: \"broadband\" in everyday speech means \"fast internet\". Here it has its technical meaning: transmission in a band away from 0 Hz.\n\nCHECKPOINT (oral): \"Why can't Wi-Fi use baseband?\" Expected: the air is shared, antennas need high frequencies, and regulations allow Wi-Fi only in specific bands away from 0 Hz.", "video": "A 74-second silent recap of the lecture. You narrate; the captions carry the key words.\n\nHow to run it: click the video to start; click again to pause. Press the right arrow to expand it to full screen (the next slide); press the left arrow to come back.\n\nTimeline:\n0:00 Title.\n0:05 Amplitude grows, then frequency rises, then phase shifts. Ask students to name each change before the caption appears.\n0:17 PAUSE & PREDICT: A signal's period is 1 ms. What is its frequency? Answer: 1 kHz.\n0:21 Time domain and frequency domain side by side: the spike follows the frequency.\n0:27 Three sine waves add into a composite signal: three spikes.\n0:33 Odd harmonics build a square wave.\n0:44 PAUSE & PREDICT: How many harmonics does a perfect square wave need? Answer: infinitely many, so infinite bandwidth.\n0:48 A channel window narrows; the received bits blur.\n1:00 PAUSE & PREDICT: To send bits twice as fast, what must happen to the bandwidth? Answer: roughly double (B ≈ N/2 grows with N).\n1:06 Recap: sine wave, Fourier, bandwidth.", "videofull": "Full-screen view of the same clip. It starts playing when this slide opens; click to pause or play. Press the left arrow to return, or the right arrow to continue.\nPause & predict answers: 0:17 → 1 kHz. 0:44 → infinitely many harmonics. 1:00 → about double the bandwidth.", "check3": "End-of-lecture review with calculators.\n\nANSWERS\nQ1: B = 800 − 200 = 600 Hz.\nQ2: 1 / (2 × 10^6) = 0.5 × 10^-6 s = 0.5 µs (500 ns).\nQ3: B ≈ N/2 = 10 Mbps / 2 = 5 MHz.\n\nExtension: \"With the first three odd harmonics?\" Expected: 5N/2 = 25 MHz.", "summary": "Summarise in two minutes. Ask a student to state each takeaway in their own words before you read it.\n\nFormulas to remember for the exam: f = 1/T, λ = c/f, B = f_high − f_low, bits per level = log2 L, bit interval = 1/N, bit length = speed × bit interval, B_min ≈ N/2.\n\nBridge to Lecture 3: today's signals were ideal. Real channels weaken signals (attenuation), change their shape (distortion) and add random disturbances (noise). Next time we measure these effects in decibels and find the maximum possible data rate of a channel.\n\nHomework idea: in Signal Lab tab 3, find the smallest bandwidth that carries the pattern 01001000 at 16 bps with no errors, and explain the result using B ≈ N/2."};
